        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The <strong>quotient rule for exponents</strong> states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:</p>",
            "readable_text": "The quotient rule for exponents states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For example, </p>",
            "readable_text": "For example,",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (a^{5})/(a^{2})|=; a^{(5 \u2212 2)}|=; a^{3}.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (a^{5})/(a^{2})|=; a^{(5 \u2212 2)}|=; a^{3}.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To see why the quotient rule is true, let&#x27;s take the above example and work it out again, the long way.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>For the two terms <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^5]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^2]</span> we have <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a]</span> and <span class=\"math\">[MATH: a^2 = a \\cdot a.]</span> If we now substitute these two expressions into <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},]</span> we get the same result:</p>",
            "readable_text": "For the two terms [MATH: a^5] and [MATH: a^2] we have [MATH: a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a] and [MATH: a^2 = a \\cdot a.] If we now substitute these two expressions into [MATH: \\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},] we get the same result:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&amp;=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&amp;=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&amp;=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &amp;=\\\na^3\n}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\na^3\n}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above.</p>",
            "readable_text": "The quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nThe quotient rule for exponents states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}]\n\nContent:\nFor example,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (a^{5})/(a^{2})|=; a^{(5 \u2212 2)}|=; a^{3}.]\n\nContent:\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nContent:\nFor the two terms [MATH: a^5] and [MATH: a^2] we have [MATH: a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a] and [MATH: a^2 = a \\cdot a.] If we now substitute these two expressions into [MATH: \\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},] we get the same result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\na^3\n}]\n\nContent:\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above."
      },
      {
