    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nThe quotient rule for exponents states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}]\n\nContent:\nFor example,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (a^{5})/(a^{2})|=; a^{(5 \u2212 2)}|=; a^{3}.]\n\nContent:\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nContent:\nFor the two terms [MATH: a^5] and [MATH: a^2] we have [MATH: a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a] and [MATH: a^2 = a \\cdot a.] If we now substitute these two expressions into [MATH: \\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},] we get the same result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\na^3\n}]\n\nContent:\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Applying the Quotient Rule for Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n[MATH: (x^{2})/(x^{3})|=; x^{(2 \u2212 3)}|=; x^{\u2212 1}]\n\nThen, we convert the negative exponent to a positive exponent by taking the reciprocal:\n\n[MATH: x^{\u2212 1}|= (1)/(x)]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3} = \\dfrac 1 x.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (a^{5})/(a^{9}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: a^{14}]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(a^{4})]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(a^{14})]\nd. [MATH: 1]\ne. [MATH: a^{4}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (z^{18})/(z^{7}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: z^{14}]\nb. [MATH: z^{11}]\nc. [MATH: z]\nd. [MATH: z^{15}]\ne. [MATH: z^{2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Applying the Quotient Rule with Negative Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: \\dfrac{z^5}{z^{-3} }.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n[MATH: (z^{5})/(z^{\u2212 3})|=; z^{5 \u2212 (\u2212 3)}|=; z^{5 + 3}|=; z^{8}]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (x^{\u2212 2})/(x^{\u2212 5}).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(x^{7})]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(x^{3})]\nc. [MATH: x^{3}]\nd. [MATH: x^{5}]\ne. [MATH: x^{7}]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (t)/(t^{\u2212 3}).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: t^{4}]\nb. [MATH: t^{\u2212 2}]\nc. [MATH: t^{2}]\nd. [MATH: t^{\u2212 4}]\ne. [MATH: t^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Applying the Quotient Rule and Product Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify the expression [MATH: \\dfrac {p^7} {p^2} \\cdot p^3 .]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst we apply the quotient rule, subtracting exponents in the fraction:\n\n[MATH: (p^{7})/(p^{2}) \u22c5 p^{3}|=; p^{(7 \u2212 2)} \u22c5 p^{3}|=; p^{5} \u22c5 p^{3}]\n\nThen we apply the product rule, adding exponents in the product:\n\n[MATH: p^{5} \u22c5 p^{3}|=; p^{(5 + 3)}|=; p^{8}]\n\nTherefore,\n\n[MATH: (p^{7})/(p^{2}) \u22c5 p^{3} = p^{8}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression\n[MATH: (x^{5})/(x^{7}) \u22c5 x^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x^{5}]\nb. [MATH: x]\nc. [MATH: x^{3}]\nd. [MATH: (1)/(x^{6})]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(x)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nSimplify the expression\n[MATH: (x^{5})/(x^{3}) \u22c5 x^{5}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: x^{7}]\nb. [MATH: x^{22}]\nc. [MATH: x^{2}]\nd. [MATH: x^{10}]\ne. [MATH: x^{5}]"
